## 1. Full Project Summary (Version B)

**Title:** Explainable Risk-Weighted Arbitration for Autonomous Cloud Remediation: A Sandbox-Validated Multi-Criteria Decision Framework (system name: SafeOpsAI)

**What it does, end to end:**

```
Fault Injection (you simulate failures)
        ↓
Prometheus (collects metrics)
        ↓
Incident Agent (detects abnormal conditions) — ALREADY BUILT
        ↓
Root Cause Agent (LLM diagnoses "why", with a CALIBRATED confidence score)
        ↓
   ┌────────────┬─────────────────┬──────────────┐
Cost Agent   Reliability Agent   Security Agent      ← score each candidate fix independently
   └────────────┴─────────────────┴──────────────┘
        ↓
Coordinator (deterministic weighted-sum math — NOT another LLM call)
   Final Score = w1·Cost + w2·Reliability + w3·Security
   Weights are configurable per deployment, and this is logged/auditable
        ↓
Sandbox Validator (applies the winning fix to an isolated replica, health-checks it)
        ↓
   PASS → Production Remediation        FAIL → discard, log, try next-best fix
        ↓
Rollback Monitor (watches post-deploy health; auto-reverts on regression)
        ↓
Decision Log Store (every score, weight, and outcome — this is what powers your paper's results)
```

**The one-sentence pitch:** most autonomous remediation systems either stop at diagnosis or pick a fix using one opaque black-box score; SafeOpsAI decomposes that decision into named, weighted, independently auditable criteria and empirically measures whether sandbox testing actually predicts production success — something the closest comparable system (PASE, 2026) doesn't do.

**What's already built (from your current implementation):** Cloud simulation (frontend/backend/DB), fault injection, Prometheus + Grafana, Incident Agent. This is your foundation — keep it as-is.

**What Version B adds on top:**
1. Root Cause Agent with logged, calibrated confidence (not just a number, but a number you *prove* is trustworthy).
2. Cost / Reliability / Security scoring agents (can be lightweight rule-based scorers, not full LLM calls — cheaper, faster, more explainable).
3. Deterministic Coordinator (weighted-sum, optionally compared against TOPSIS as an ablation).
4. Sandbox Validator — a real isolated environment where a fix is tried before touching "production" (your simulated prod).
5. Rollback controller.
6. Decision Logger — the data backbone for your entire evaluation section.
7. Evaluation harness — baselines, ablations, weight-sensitivity sweep, calibration curve (this is what makes it publishable, not optional).

**Deliverables for your final-year submission:** working system + IEEE-formatted paper draft + decision-log dataset (small but real) + your presentation dashboard.

