## 2. Why Python — Justified Against Alternatives

You asked specifically why Python over other languages, so here's the honest technical reasoning, not just "it's what I know":

| Criterion | Python (your choice) | Java/Spring Boot (your original spec) | Go | Node.js/TypeScript |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **AI/LLM ecosystem** | Best-in-class — LangChain, LangGraph, Ollama SDKs, HuggingFace, all native | Weak — most LLM tooling has Python-first SDKs; Java bindings lag or are community-maintained | Very weak for LLM orchestration | Decent (LangChain.js exists) but noticeably behind Python in maturity/examples |
| **FastAPI vs Spring Boot for this use case** | FastAPI is async-native, lightweight, auto-generates OpenAPI docs, ideal for a service that's mostly I/O-bound (waiting on LLM calls, DB queries, Prometheus scrapes) | Spring Boot is heavier, more boilerplate, better suited to large enterprise teams/long-lived systems than a research prototype | Would require hand-rolling much more (no direct FastAPI-equivalent for LLM work) | Express/Nest is comparable in lightness but again loses on AI tooling |
| **Data/metrics analysis for your evaluation harness** | pandas, scipy, matplotlib/seaborn are the de facto standard for exactly the statistical analysis your Step 6 evaluation needs (t-tests, calibration curves, plots) | Possible but far more verbose; you'd likely export data and analyze in Python anyway | Would still likely export to Python for analysis | Same — would still lean on Python for analysis |
| **Prometheus/Grafana integration** | prometheus_client library is mature and simple | Micrometer (Spring's metrics library) is also solid — this is actually a wash | Good native support | Decent support |
| **Team/individual velocity for a final-year timeline** | Fastest to prototype, iterate, and debug for a solo/small-team project under deadline pressure | Slower iteration due to verbosity and build overhead (Maven/Gradle, class boilerplate) | Fast to build, but LLM orchestration is the weak point for your project specifically | Fast, but same LLM tooling gap as above |
| **Where Java/Go would actually win** | — | Better raw throughput, stronger typing discipline, better fit if this were a **production-grade enterprise system** handling high concurrent load | Best raw performance/concurrency for the Incident Agent's polling loop specifically, if that becomes a bottleneck | Good if your team is already a JS/TS shop |

**Bottom line for your paper's "Implementation" section:** justify Python specifically because your core contribution (LLM-driven agents + MCDM decision layer + statistical evaluation) sits squarely in Python's strongest ecosystem — LangChain/LangGraph for agent orchestration, FastAPI for async service handling, pandas/scipy for the evaluation harness. Say explicitly that the original spec allowed Java/Spring Boot, and that Python was chosen deliberately because the *research contribution* (not the CRUD layer) is AI/agent-centric — that's a legitimate, defensible engineering decision, not a shortcut. Reviewers care whether you can justify tooling choices; you can.

