## 3. This IS a Cloud Computing Project — Full Tech Stack (Including Hosting)

You're right that the earlier breakdown was mostly your *local simulation* stack. Since you want to actually **host** this (not just run `docker compose up` locally), here's the complete cloud computing stack across every layer:

### Layer 1 — Application (what you already have)

| Component | Technology | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Backend API | Python + FastAPI | Async, LLM-tooling-native, fast to iterate (see justification above) |
| Frontend | HTML/CSS/JS + Nginx | Lightweight; Nginx also doubles as reverse proxy in production |
| Database | PostgreSQL | Relational integrity for incidents/agent_decisions/remediation_actions tables; mature, well-supported on every cloud provider as a managed service |
| Agent Framework | LangChain / LangGraph | Native support for multi-agent orchestration, tool calling, and stateful agent graphs — directly matches your Root Cause → Cost/Reliability/Security → Coordinator flow |
| LLM Runtime | Ollama (Llama 3 / Mistral, local) | Free, no API cost for hundreds of experimental runs (you'll run 60+ trials — OpenAI API costs would add up fast), and it demonstrates the system works without dependency on a commercial LLM vendor — a genuine point in your paper's favor for reproducibility |

### Layer 2 — Containerization & Orchestration (local dev)

| Component | Technology | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Containers | Docker | Standard, portable, matches what you already have |
| Local orchestration | Docker Compose | Fine for development and even for demoing — but **not what you deploy publicly** |

### Layer 3 — Cloud Hosting (this is the part you're missing, and where "cloud computing domain" actually gets proven)

This is the layer that makes the project *actually* a cloud computing project rather than "a Docker Compose app I built once." For hosting, you have two realistic paths depending on budget/scope:

**Option A — Simple, cheap, good for a demo + paper (recommended for final year):**

| Component | Technology | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Cloud provider | Any of AWS, GCP, Azure — all offer free-tier/student credits | GCP and AWS both have generous student/education credit programs; pick whichever your institution has a tie-in with |
| Compute | A single VM (AWS EC2 t3.medium / GCP e2-medium or similar) running Docker Compose | Cheapest way to get your existing stack "live" without a full Kubernetes rebuild — you SSH in, `docker compose up -d`, done |
| Reverse proxy / TLS | Nginx + Let's Encrypt (Certbot) | Free HTTPS, standard practice |
| Managed database (optional upgrade) | Move PostgreSQL to a managed service (AWS RDS / GCP Cloud SQL) instead of a container | More realistic "cloud" architecture, demonstrates separation of stateful vs. stateless components — a genuinely relevant point for an AIOps paper since your fault injection could target either layer differently |
| Monitoring | Prometheus + Grafana still self-hosted on the same VM, or Grafana Cloud free tier | Either is fine; self-hosted is simpler for full control over fault injection visibility |

**Option B — Closer to "real" cloud-native, stronger for the paper's credibility (if you have time):**

| Component | Technology | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Orchestration | Kubernetes (managed: GKE Autopilot / EKS / AKS, or a cheap alternative like k3s on a VM) | This is what almost every paper you're being compared against (PASE, RCAgent, mABC) implicitly assumes as the deployment context — microservice fault injection and remediation research is written *for* Kubernetes-style environments |
| Service mesh (optional, adds real fault-injection realism) | Istio or Linkerd | Lets you inject network-level faults (latency, packet loss) more realistically than app-level fault flags — strengthens your fault injection story significantly if you have time |
| Infrastructure as Code | Terraform | Lets you version and reproduce your cloud environment — also directly citable as good research practice (reproducibility) |
| CI/CD | GitHub Actions | Free for public repos, straightforward to wire up build → test → deploy |
| Secrets/config | Kubernetes Secrets + ConfigMaps, or a cloud provider's secret manager | Standard practice, worth mentioning briefly in your paper's implementation section |

**My honest recommendation:** go with **Option A** for your working deployment (a single hosted VM running your existing Docker Compose stack, with a managed PostgreSQL if you want the extra polish), and mention Kubernetes/service-mesh migration explicitly as **future work** in your paper rather than trying to build it now. This is a real, common, and respected framing in systems papers — "current work is validated on Docker Compose; production-grade deployment would use Kubernetes with service mesh-level fault injection, which we leave to future work" is a normal, honest sentence to write, and it avoids you burning your remaining timeline on Kubernetes debugging instead of the actual research contribution (the Coordinator + evaluation).

### Layer 4 — Where this appears in your paper

Add a short **"Deployment Architecture"** subsection to your Methodology section, roughly:
- State you deployed on [AWS/GCP/Azure] using a single VM + Docker Compose, with PostgreSQL as [containerized/managed].
- State this validates the system works in an actual cloud environment (not just localhost), addressing external validity concerns a reviewer would otherwise raise.
- Explicitly scope Kubernetes-based multi-node deployment as future work — this shows awareness of production-scale concerns without overclaiming what you built.

